---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const join = (path: string) => `${baseUrl.replace(/\/$/, '')}${path}`;
---

<ArticleLayout
  title="AWS multi-account skill credentials"
  description="Named profiles, environment-derived defaults, prod-acknowledgment guard, and an sts:GetCallerIdentity audit banner before any AWS call."
  eyebrow="Outcome"
  lede="We proved that a single SSO login can safely serve multiple AWS-touching skills in one agent prompt, with a hard prod-acknowledgment guard and a per-invocation audit banner, using only named profiles and standard boto3."
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: join('/') },
    { label: 'Outcomes', href: join('/outcomes/') },
    { label: 'AWS skill creds' },
  ]}
>
  <Callout variant="info" label="TL;DR">
    <ul>
      <li>Decision 0016 established a five-rule named-profile convention that lets multiple AWS-touching skills share one SSO session without shell-global credential collision.</li>
      <li>The reference implementation shipped as <code>skills/verify-user-authorization/</code>, exercising all five rules against live DynamoDB tables in dev and prod.</li>
      <li>An auto-load rule at <code>.claude/rules/aws-skill-credentials.md</code> enforces the convention for every sub-agent that authors or invokes an AWS-touching skill.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Before Decision 0016, AWS-touching skills relied on pasting three shell-global environment
    variables (<code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code>, <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code>,
    <code>AWS_SESSION_TOKEN</code>) from the SSO console. That works for one skill at a time;
    it breaks the moment a coordinator prompt invokes two skills targeting two different AWS
    accounts, because only one set of env-var credentials can be active in the shell at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Decision 0016 replaced the env-var pattern with named AWS profiles constructed via
    <code>boto3.Session(profile_name=...)</code>. A single <code>[sso-session linq]</code> block
    in <code>~/.aws/config</code>, combined with one <code>aws sso login --sso-session linq</code>
    per day, warms every profile that references that session. Two skills in one prompt compose
    seamlessly with no shared shell state.
  </p>
  <p>
    The convention defines five rules, captured in <code>.claude/rules/aws-skill-credentials.md</code>
    and enforced as an auto-load rule for any sub-agent that touches a skill importing
    <code>boto3</code> or naming a LINQ AWS resource. Every AWS-touching SKILL.md must include
    an <code>## AWS profiles</code> section with the canonical stanza, so operators who learn the
    layout in one skill recognize it in every other.
  </p>
  <p>
    The forward path is the centralized platform MCP IdentityBroker described in Decision 0015.
    When that broker reaches GA, the named-profile resolution block in each skill becomes a
    single-block swap for broker-issued short-lived credentials. Every other layer—argparse,
    audit banner, prod guardrail, SKILL.md contract—stays unchanged.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-we-shipped">What we shipped</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Decision 0016</strong> — the canonical ADR documenting all seven rules of the
      named-profile convention, the alternatives considered, and the migration seam toward the
      future IdentityBroker.
      (<a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0016-aws-multi-account-skill-credentials.md">docs/decisions/0016&#8209;aws&#8209;multi&#8209;account&#8209;skill&#8209;credentials.md</a>)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Auto-load rule</strong> at <code>.claude/rules/aws-skill-credentials.md</code> — loads
      whenever an agent authors or invokes an AWS-touching skill, enforcing the named-profile rule,
      environment-toggle rule, prod-acknowledgment rule, audit-banner rule, and three-phase error rule.
      (<a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0016-aws-multi-account-skill-credentials.md">.claude/rules/aws-skill-credentials.md</a>)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reference implementation</strong> at <code>skills/verify-user-authorization/</code> —
      canonical Python script exercising all five rules, including the <code>sts:GetCallerIdentity</code>
      audit banner, three-phase error handling, and the <code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code>
      guardrail against live DynamoDB.
      (<a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md">skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md</a>)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Wiki concept page</strong> at <code>knowledge/wiki/concepts/aws-skill-credential-pattern.md</code> —
      mirrors Decision 0016 for retrieval by future agents without navigating the full ADR.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="what-we-learned">What we learned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Conflating "which AWS account" with "which dataset" is the root cause of dev-creds-on-prod-data accidents; a single <code>--environment</code> flag that derives both the profile and the resource names closes that gap mechanically.</li>
    <li>The prod-acknowledgment flag (<code>--i-understand-this-is-prod</code>, no short alias) forces a deliberate choice in agent-generated prompts, not just human ones—agents drafting prod invocations must include it explicitly, surfacing ambiguity before the call is made.</li>
    <li>The <code>sts:GetCallerIdentity</code> audit banner doubles as a credential-validity check: an expired SSO token, a missing profile, or a misconfigured ambient chain surfaces before any downstream DynamoDB call, not after a confusing <code>AccessDenied</code>.</li>
    <li>Designing the credential seam at <code>boto3.Session(...)</code> means the eventual migration to broker-issued credentials is a single-block change per skill; everything above that line is already reusable.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="whats-next">What's next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Add an <code>## AWS profiles</code> section to every new AWS-touching skill as it lands, reusing the headings verbatim from <code>skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md</code>.</li>
    <li>When Decision 0015's centralized platform MCP IdentityBroker reaches GA (M4+), evaluate migrating each skill off named-profile resolution to broker-issued short-lived credentials — the <code>boto3.Session(...)</code> seam makes that a per-skill, single-block change.</li>
    <li>Add a cross-account AssumeRole path for skills that must touch two accounts in one invocation — the named-profile pattern covers the current case (one skill, one account); AssumeRole chaining is deferred until a concrete need arises.</li>
    <li>Provision an <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> secret in CI and re-enable <code>evals/run.py --ci</code> gating; until then, schema validation in <code>tests/test_schemas.py</code> catches structural regressions on every PR.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deep-dive">Deep dive</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pillar:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/pillars/5-skills-management.md">Pillar 5 — Skills management</a></li>
    <li><strong>Decision:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/docs/decisions/0016-aws-multi-account-skill-credentials.md">Decision 0016 — AWS multi-account, multi-environment credential convention for skills</a></li>
    <li><strong>Source:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/shannoncarver/hackathon-may-2026/blob/main/skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md"><code>skills/verify-user-authorization/SKILL.md</code></a></li>
    <li><strong>Tutorial (worked example for operators):</strong> <a href={join('/tutorials/verify-user-authorization/')}>verify-user-authorization tutorial</a></li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
